body:
Hmm.. I might be missing something, but I don't think it's true that `imgAttributes` will actually always be (`{ src: '...', srcSet: undefined, sizes: undefined }`) when JS is disabled

On my end, I tested this by logging `imgAttributes`, and its `srcSet` value is not `undefined`:

```javascript
<noscript>
        {/* only logs to terminal, because JavaScript is disabled in browser */}
        {console.log(imgAttributes)}
        <img
          {...rest}
          {...imgAttributes}
          src={src}
          decoding="async"
          sizes={sizes}
          style={{ ...imgStyle, visibility: 'inherit' }}
          className={className}
        />
</noscript>
```

The result, on the `image-component` example, is:

```javascript
{
  'data-src': '/_next/image?url=%2Fmountains.jpg&w=3840&q=75',
  'data-srcset': '/_next/image?url=%2Fmountains.jpg&w=750&q=75 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fmountains.jpg&w=1920&q=75 2x, /_next/image?url=%2Fmountains.jpg&w=3840&q=75 3x'
}
```

But actually, on this point, I just noticed that we weren't adding `srcset` on the `<noscript><img /></noscript>` element.

So, I will update this PR so that it looks something like this:

```javascript
<noscript>
        <img
          {...rest}
          {...imgAttributes}
          srcSet={imgSrcSet}
          src={src}
          decoding="async"
          sizes={sizes}
          style={{ ...imgStyle, visibility: 'inherit' }}
          className={className}
        />
</noscript>
```

I tested it locally and it now correctly uses `srcset` to request the correct sized image, based on the viewport, which it wasn't doing previously:

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/18399089/110554578-fd1fce00-8132-11eb-8b70-4029504d9e38.mp4

What do you think?

